(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pharm-e3-drill-pulmonary"]={"title":"Pulmonary Drugs Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antibiotic is preferred for acute bronchitis when Mycoplasma is documented, because of macrolide resistance?","choices":["Doxycycline","Azithromycin","Levofloxacin","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Doxycycline is the preferred choice when Mycoplasma is documented in acute bronchitis, because of macrolide resistance. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which drug is listed with doxycycline as second-line outpatient therapy for community-acquired pneumonia without resistance risk?","choices":["Clarithromycin","Minocycline","Cefpodoxime","Moxifloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With no risk of resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae, a macrolide is first line and doxycycline or minocycline is second line. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which respiratory fluoroquinolone is an outpatient choice for community-acquired pneumonia with risk factors for resistant pneumococcus?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Minocycline","Moxifloxacin","Azithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With risk factors for resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae, a beta-lactam plus a macrolide or a respiratory fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin, moxifloxacin) is used. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which penicillin, given intravenously in high dose, is a beta-lactam choice for inpatient community-acquired pneumonia?","choices":["Piperacillin/tazobactam","Amoxicillin","Ertapenem","Ampicillin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inpatient (non-intensive care) pneumonia gets an intravenous beta-lactam: cefotaxime, ceftriaxone or high-dose ampicillin, plus a macrolide. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which third-generation cephalosporin is a limited-spectrum choice for nosocomial pneumonia without multidrug-resistant risk factors?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Cefepime","Ceftazidime","Cefuroxime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With no risk factors for multidrug-resistant pathogens, ceftriaxone (a third-generation cephalosporin) is a limited-spectrum option. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which carbapenem has limited activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa?","choices":["Meropenem","Ertapenem","Imipenem/cilastatin","Cefepime"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ertapenem has limited activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which is why it is listed as a narrow-spectrum carbapenem. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which antipseudomonal carbapenem is used with caution in patients with a seizure history?","choices":["Meropenem","Ertapenem","Imipenem/cilastatin","Piperacillin/tazobactam"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Imipenem/cilastatin (Primaxin) is used with caution in patients with a seizure history; meropenem carries less seizure risk. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which carbapenem is listed with less risk for seizures?","choices":["Imipenem/cilastatin","Ertapenem","Ceftazidime","Meropenem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Meropenem (Merrem) is the antipseudomonal carbapenem with less risk for seizures than imipenem/cilastatin. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which antipseudomonal cephalosporin is a fourth-generation cephalosporin?","choices":["Cefepime","Ceftazidime","Ceftriaxone","Cefotaxime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cefepime (Maxipime) is the fourth-generation antipseudomonal cephalosporin; like ceftazidime, it requires renal adjustment. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which antipseudomonal penicillin combination is sold as Zosyn?","choices":["Ticarcillin/clavulanate","Piperacillin/tazobactam","Imipenem/cilastatin","Ampicillin/sulbactam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Piperacillin/tazobactam (Zosyn) is an antipseudomonal penicillin with a beta-lactamase inhibitor; it requires renal adjustment. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which antipseudomonal fluoroquinolone is not listed as a respiratory fluoroquinolone for community-acquired pneumonia?","choices":["Levofloxacin","Moxifloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Tobramycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ciprofloxacin (Cipro) and levofloxacin are the antipseudomonal fluoroquinolones; only levofloxacin also appears with moxifloxacin as a respiratory fluoroquinolone. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slides 27, 14"},{"q":"Which antibiotic gives great methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage with no dose adjustment for renal function?","choices":["Vancomycin","Gentamicin","Cefepime","Linezolid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Linezolid (Zyvox) has great methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage, intravenous and oral forms, good lung penetration and no renal adjustment. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which drug is primarily used for empiric methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage, with a trough drawn before the third or fourth dose?","choices":["Vancomycin","Linezolid","Amikacin","Meropenem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is primarily used for empiric methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage; its trough is drawn before the third or fourth dose, at steady state. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slides 29, 25"},{"q":"Which antibiotic class uses extended-interval (once daily or longer) dosing because of poor lung penetration?","choices":["Antipseudomonal fluoroquinolones","Aminoglycosides","Antipseudomonal carbapenems","Antipseudomonal cephalosporins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin) use extended-interval dosing: poor lung penetration means bigger doses for higher concentrations. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Broad nosocomial coverage pairs an antipseudomonal beta-lactam with which second gram-negative class?","choices":["Antipseudomonal carbapenems","Third-generation cephalosporins","Aminoglycosides","Macrolides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Broad coverage is an antipseudomonal beta-lactam PLUS an antipseudomonal fluoroquinolone or an aminoglycoside, PLUS vancomycin or linezolid. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which drug supplies the gram-positive coverage in a broad regimen of meropenem plus amikacin?","choices":["Piperacillin-tazobactam","Gentamicin","Levofloxacin","Vancomycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Meropenem (antipseudomonal carbapenem) plus amikacin (aminoglycoside) is the gram-negative pair; vancomycin or linezolid adds gram-positive coverage. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slides 23, 25, 28"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug causes peripheral neuritis from pyridoxine deficiency, so pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is given?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid causes peripheral neuritis through pyridoxine deficiency, so pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is given with it. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug is used alone for prophylaxis?","choices":["Doxycycline","Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid is used alone for prophylaxis and in combination for active disease. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug is metabolically activated by bacterial catalase-peroxidase?","choices":["Pyrazinamide","Rifampin","Isoniazid","Ethambutol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid is metabolically activated by bacterial catalase-peroxidase; resistance comes from a mutation in the gene for that enzyme. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug can cause a lupus-like syndrome?","choices":["Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide","Isoniazid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid's allergic reactions include a lupus-like syndrome, alongside hepatitis, peripheral neuritis and central nervous system effects. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug is excreted mostly as an acetylated metabolite, with fast and slow acetylators?","choices":["Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Rifampin","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid is 75 to 95 percent excreted as an acetylated metabolite, and its half-life differs between fast and slow acetylators. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug colors tears, sweat and urine orange?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin colors secretions orange (tears, sweat, urine); patients should be told to expect it. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug induces cytochrome P450 enzymes, lowering anticoagulants and contraceptives?","choices":["Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Rifampin","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin induces cytochrome P450 enzymes (CYP1A2, CYP2C9, CYP3A4), affecting anticoagulants, contraceptives, digoxin, ketoconazole and theophylline. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug inhibits bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase?","choices":["Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide","Rifampin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase by forming a complex with the enzyme, with no effect on the human enzyme. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug is also used for prophylaxis of meningococcal disease?","choices":["Rifampin","Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin's primary use is tuberculosis, with meningococcal disease prophylaxis and endocarditis also listed. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug causes a flu-like hypersensitivity syndrome in about 20 percent of patients?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin's hypersensitivity is a flu-like syndrome in about 20 percent of patients; it also causes jaundice, hepatitis and gastrointestinal upset. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug decreases red-green color discrimination?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ethambutol causes visual disturbances, decreased red-green discrimination and decreased visual acuity. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug is 75 percent excreted unchanged, so its dose is adjusted in renal disease?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Pyrazinamide","Ethambutol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ethambutol is 75 percent excreted unchanged, so the dose is adjusted in renal disease; resistance develops quickly if it is used alone. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which drug weakens the mycobacterial cell wall so other tuberculosis agents gain better access?","choices":["Ethambutol","Rifampin","Pyrazinamide","Gentamicin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ethambutol inhibits mycolic acid incorporation into membranes; the weakened cell wall lets other agents in more easily. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug is converted to pyrazinoic acid, lowering the pH so bacteria cannot grow?","choices":["Isoniazid","Pyrazinamide","Ethambutol","Rifampin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide is converted to pyrazinoic acid, which lowers the pH so the bacteria cannot grow; it also enters macrophages. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug is stopped after two months specifically to avoid liver toxicity?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Pyrazinamide","Ethambutol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide shortens combined therapy and is given for the first two months only, to avoid liver toxicity. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which tuberculosis drug is to be avoided in pregnancy?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide's adverse effect list ends with avoid in pregnancy, after hepatitis and hyperuricemia. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which drug inhibits uric acid excretion so that all patients taking it become hyperuricemic?","choices":["Pyrazinamide","Isoniazid","Rifampin","Vancomycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide inhibits uric acid excretion, and all patients become hyperuricemic; it also causes hepatitis in about 15 percent. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which anaphylaxis drug acts on both alpha and beta receptors?","choices":["Albuterol","Epinephrine","Diphenhydramine","Famotidine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, an endogenous catecholamine, acts on alpha receptors (vasoconstriction, less vascular permeability) and beta receptors (bronchial relaxation). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which anaphylaxis drug, in overdose, can cause cerebrovascular hemorrhage, myocardial infarction and pulmonary edema?","choices":["Methylprednisolone","Hydroxyzine","Epinephrine","Famotidine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine overdose can lead to cerebrovascular hemorrhage, myocardial infarction and pulmonary edema; the intramuscular route is preferred unless the patient is coding. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which drug used in anaphylaxis is a histamine H2 blocker?","choices":["Diphenhydramine","Hydroxyzine","Albuterol","Famotidine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Famotidine is the histamine H2 blocker; it blocks the systemic (vasodilation) and gastrointestinal effects such as increased gastric acid. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which histamine H1 blocker is listed with diphenhydramine for anaphylaxis?","choices":["Hydroxyzine","Famotidine","Albuterol","Prednisone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The histamine H1 blockers are diphenhydramine and hydroxyzine; they relieve rash, pruritus and hives. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which anaphylaxis drug blocks inflammatory mediators at the cellular level but takes several hours to work?","choices":["Epinephrine","Methylprednisolone","Diphenhydramine","Albuterol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroids such as methylprednisolone (intramuscular, intravenous) block inflammatory mediator production and work on all aspects, but take several hours. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which short-acting beta-2 agonist is sold as Xopenex?","choices":["Albuterol","Formoterol","Levalbuterol","Salmeterol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Levalbuterol (Xopenex) is the short-acting beta-2 agonist listed with albuterol (ProAir, Ventolin). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which long-acting beta-2 agonist is sold as Serevent?","choices":["Formoterol","Levalbuterol","Albuterol","Salmeterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Salmeterol (Serevent) is a long-acting beta-2 agonist; long-acting agents are not to be used alone for asthma. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Foradil is the brand name of which long-acting beta-2 agonist?","choices":["Formoterol","Salmeterol","Levalbuterol","Tiotropium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Formoterol (Foradil) is a long-acting beta-2 agonist; it is paired with budesonide (Symbicort) and mometasone (Dulera). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which beta-2 agonist is sold as ProAir and Ventolin?","choices":["Levalbuterol","Albuterol","Salmeterol","Ipratropium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Albuterol (ProAir, Ventolin) is a short-acting beta-2 agonist, listed with levalbuterol (Xopenex). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slides 70, 86"},{"q":"Which drug class must never be used alone for asthma?","choices":["Inhaled corticosteroids","Leukotriene modifiers","Long-acting beta-2 agonists","Short-acting beta-2 agonists"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Long-acting beta-2 agonists (formoterol, salmeterol) are not to be used alone for asthma; they are paired with an inhaled corticosteroid. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slides 70, 67"},{"q":"Which bronchodilator class raises cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) by stimulating a receptor on airway smooth muscle?","choices":["Methylxanthines","Anticholinergics","Leukotriene modifiers","Beta-2 agonists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta-2 agonists stimulate the beta-2 receptor, increasing cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) and relaxing airway smooth muscle. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which bronchodilator is a nonspecific phosphodiesterase inhibitor with a narrow therapeutic index?","choices":["Theophylline","Albuterol","Ipratropium","Montelukast"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline, a methylxanthine, is a nonspecific phosphodiesterase inhibitor with a narrow therapeutic index, so its plasma level is monitored. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which bronchodilator antagonizes the adenosine A2B receptor?","choices":["Tiotropium","Theophylline","Salmeterol","Ketamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline inhibits phosphodiesterase (raising cyclic adenosine and guanosine monophosphate), antagonizes the adenosine A2B receptor and has anti-inflammatory actions. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which drug is the intravenous form of theophylline?","choices":["Ipratropium","Levalbuterol","Aminophylline","Magnesium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aminophylline is the intravenous form of theophylline, the methylxanthine. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which short-acting anticholinergic lasts up to 8 hours?","choices":["Tiotropium","Albuterol","Salmeterol","Ipratropium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ipratropium (Atrovent) is the short-acting anticholinergic, lasting up to 8 hours; it blocks acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which long-acting anticholinergic is sold as Spiriva?","choices":["Tiotropium","Ipratropium","Formoterol","Mometasone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tiotropium (Spiriva) is the long-acting anticholinergic, lasting 24 hours or more. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which drug class causes the side effects remembered as dry as a bone, blind as a bat and red as a beet?","choices":["Beta-2 agonists","Anticholinergics","Methylxanthines","Inhaled corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anticholinergic side effects: dry mouth and eyes, blurry vision and mydriasis, flushing, fever, delirium, constipation, urinary retention and tachycardia. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which leukotriene modifier antagonizes the leukotriene D4 (cysteinyl leukotriene 1) receptor?","choices":["Cromolyn sodium","Nedocromil","Montelukast","Omalizumab"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Montelukast (Singulair), an oral leukotriene antagonist, blocks the leukotriene D4 receptor that mediates inflammation, bronchoconstriction and mucus. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which asthma controller is effective in aspirin-induced asthma?","choices":["Omalizumab","Theophylline","Nedocromil","Montelukast"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Montelukast, the leukotriene D4 receptor antagonist, is listed as effective in aspirin-induced asthma. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which inhaled mast cell stabilizer is sold as Tilade?","choices":["Nedocromil","Cromolyn sodium","Montelukast","Mometasone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nedocromil (Tilade) is an inhaled mast cell stabilizer that may take up to 4 weeks to work and must be taken regularly. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which asthma controller class must be taken before the antigen insult and may take up to 4 weeks to work?","choices":["Inhaled corticosteroids","Mast cell stabilizers","Leukotriene modifiers","Short-acting beta-2 agonists"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mast cell stabilizers (cromolyn sodium, nedocromil) must be taken before the antigen insult and regularly; they may take up to 4 weeks to work. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which asthma drug is a humanized monoclonal antibody that binds circulating immunoglobulin E (IgE)?","choices":["Mepolizumab","Dupilumab","Omalizumab","Reslizumab"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Omalizumab (Xolair), given subcutaneously, binds circulating immunoglobulin E, which decreases high-affinity receptors on mast cells and prevents degranulation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which asthma biologic is sold as Dupixent?","choices":["Mepolizumab","Reslizumab","Omalizumab","Dupilumab"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dupilumab (Dupixent) is the anti-interleukin-4 biologic, used with the anti-interleukin-5 drugs for the eosinophilic phenotype. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which asthma biologic is sold as Nucala?","choices":["Mepolizumab","Dupilumab","Omalizumab","Reslizumab"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mepolizumab (Nucala) is in the anti-interleukin-5 group, used for the eosinophilic phenotype. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which asthma biologic is sold as Fasenra?","choices":["Dupilumab","Benralizumab","Omalizumab","Reslizumab"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Benralizumab (Fasenra) is listed in the anti-interleukin-5 group for the eosinophilic phenotype. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which inhaled corticosteroid is sold as Flovent?","choices":["Budesonide","Beclomethasone","Fluticasone","Mometasone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fluticasone (Flovent) is an inhaled corticosteroid; with salmeterol it makes Advair. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which inhaled corticosteroid is sold as Pulmicort?","choices":["Fluticasone","Flunisolide","Triamcinolone","Budesonide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Budesonide (Pulmicort) is an inhaled corticosteroid; with formoterol it makes Symbicort. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which inhaled corticosteroid is sold as QVAR?","choices":["Beclomethasone","Budesonide","Mometasone","Fluticasone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beclomethasone (QVAR) is an inhaled corticosteroid; like the others it can cause oral candidiasis (thrush). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which inhaled corticosteroid is sold as Asmanex?","choices":["Fluticasone","Mometasone","Flunisolide","Beclomethasone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mometasone (Asmanex) is an inhaled corticosteroid; with formoterol it makes Dulera. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which combination inhaler pairs salmeterol with fluticasone?","choices":["Symbicort","Dulera","Advair","Serevent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Advair is salmeterol/fluticasone, a long-acting beta-2 agonist with an inhaled corticosteroid. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which combination inhaler pairs formoterol with budesonide?","choices":["Dulera","Advair","Pulmicort","Symbicort"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symbicort is formoterol/budesonide, a long-acting beta-2 agonist with an inhaled corticosteroid. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which combination inhaler pairs formoterol with mometasone?","choices":["Dulera","Symbicort","Advair","Asmanex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dulera is formoterol/mometasone, a long-acting beta-2 agonist with an inhaled corticosteroid. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which asthma drug class causes oral candidiasis (thrush) and, at high dose, suppresses growth in children?","choices":["Leukotriene modifiers","Inhaled corticosteroids","Mast cell stabilizers","Long-acting beta-2 agonists"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled corticosteroids cause oral candidiasis (thrush), suppress growth in children at high dose and can inhibit the immune system. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which systemic corticosteroid comes as an oral liquid, sold as Ora-Pred?","choices":["Prednisone","Methylprednisolone","Prednisolone","Dexamethasone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prednisolone (Ora-Pred) is the oral liquid; prednisone (Deltasone) is the oral tablet. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which systemic corticosteroid is sold as Decadron?","choices":["Methylprednisolone","Prednisolone","Prednisone","Dexamethasone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dexamethasone (Decadron) is given by mouth or parenterally; systemic steroids are kept for exacerbations. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which drug used in status asthmaticus for an intubated patient is an NMDA (N-methyl-D-aspartate) receptor antagonist?","choices":["Ketamine","Methylprednisolone","Albuterol","Aminophylline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ketamine, a dissociative anesthetic used if the patient is intubated, antagonizes the N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor and increases catecholamine release, causing bronchodilation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which drug used in status asthmaticus can cause an emergence phenomenon?","choices":["Albuterol","Ketamine","Methylprednisolone","Aminophylline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ketamine's adverse reactions are emergence phenomenon, hypertension, tachycardia and vomiting. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which drug class is the regular treatment for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease group C?","choices":["Long-acting beta-2 agonists","Short-acting bronchodilators","Long-acting muscarinic antagonists","Inhaled corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Group C (less symptomatic, high risk) gets regular treatment with a long-acting muscarinic antagonist, with a short-acting beta-2 agonist for relief. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which smoking cessation drug is a partial nicotine receptor agonist?","choices":["Bupropion","Nicotine patch","Nicotine gum","Varenicline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Varenicline (Chantix) is a partial nicotine receptor agonist, acting to a lesser degree than nicotine. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which smoking cessation drug is listed with a black box warning for neuropsychiatric events?","choices":["Varenicline","Nicotine patch","Nicotine gum","Nicotine inhaler"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Varenicline is listed with a black box warning for neuropsychiatric events (depression, suicidal ideation), plus nausea, sleep disturbance and headache. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which smoking cessation drug is started one week before the quit date and continued for 12 weeks?","choices":["Nicotine patch","Varenicline","Nicotine spray","Bupropion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Varenicline is started one week before the quit date and continued for 12 weeks. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which smoking cessation drug inhibits the uptake of norepinephrine and dopamine?","choices":["Varenicline","Nicotine patch","Bupropion","Nicotine inhaler"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bupropion SR (sustained release; Zyban) is postulated to mimic nicotine's effects because it inhibits norepinephrine and dopamine uptake. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which smoking cessation drug is avoided in seizure disorders and in patients taking monoamine oxidase inhibitors?","choices":["Varenicline","Nicotine patch","Nicotine gum","Bupropion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bupropion SR (sustained release) is avoided in seizure disorders and with monoamine oxidase inhibitors, and used with caution with tricyclic antidepressants and venlafaxine. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which nicotine replacement product is nicotine polacrilex?","choices":["Nicotine gum","Nicotine patch","Nicotine spray","Nicotine inhaler"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nicotine polacrilex is the gum; the transdermal system is the patch, with a spray and an inhaler also available. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 104"}]};
